WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.649 --> 00:00:07.650
In this video, we're going to add a sound cue to our level in order to be able to play background music.

2
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:12.029
There are a couple different ways we can work with audio files inside of Unreal,

3
00:00:12.029 --> 00:00:21.030
but the simplest way is to place a sound cue actor into a level that will play a specific audio file.

4
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:29.579
These assets also allow you to specify attenuation settings like falloff and reverb and a million other things.

5
00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:36.090
We are going to be placing a sound cue that is included with the starter content.

6
00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:40.149
There we go. Audio, we can see a list here.

7
00:00:40.149 --> 00:00:45.149
Lots of different explosions, but we are going to pick starter music one.

8
00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:50.310
And so we can see here that they've already included a starter music sound cue for us.

9
00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:55.380
We can drag that into our level. I'll put that up a little higher.

10
00:00:55.979 --> 00:01:01.299
We'll go back to selection mode and look at our details.

11
00:01:01.299 --> 00:01:07.170
And we can see we're going to have our volume set to one.

12
00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:12.170
Attenuation, we're just going to use defaults. We're not going to worry with creating a custom setting for that.

13
00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:23.019
Now when we press play, we hear our music.

14
00:01:23.019 --> 00:01:31.019
One thing to note, though, is that Unreal is also currently switching over to the new high performance procedural audio engine meta sounds.

15
00:01:31.019 --> 00:01:39.019
So you might be seeing more and more of that as you explore working with audio inside of Unreal going forward.

16
00:01:39.019 --> 00:01:45.269
In the next video, we'll look at how we can package our game into an executable that other people can install.
